00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
two weeks of protests by angry eastern  sudan tribes have brought operations at  port sudan to a standstill  the protesters from the media and  hadandawa tribes have blocked roads and  exits to the port forcing it to close  this in protest at what they say is the  region's lack of political power  and poor economic conditions  the government just takes all the tax  from our ports  without leaving us anything sudan is not  held together by war or rule of the gun  we need them to wholeheartedly talk with  us as we have genuine grievances  the protesters are also angry with a  deal the transitional government signed  with sudan's rebel groups in october  last year  in the south sudanese capital juba  widely known as the juba accord the deal  includes a wealth sharing agreement as  well as reparations for those hacked by  the country's long conflict  it also promises that a ton of refugees  displaced by fighting assassin  we are totally against the juba accord  we were not invited and the whole  process was hijacked by foreigners the  government knows who our leaders are too  well let them start talking with them if  they want this blockade lifted  sudan's transitional government has  pledged to work on a political process  to resolve the problems of eastern sudan  but it's adding the protesters who have  blockaded this pot  to lift it immediately the government  has also said the country is on the  verge of running out of essential  medicines fuel and wheat the protesters  however deny they've been holding back  shipments of food and medical supplies  the statement from the government was  only meant to gain sympathy from the  public throughout the protests we  haven't stopped even a single shipment  of food or medical supplies from leaving  the port  the demonstrators agreed last sunday to  allow the resumption of exports of crude  oil from landlocked south sudan through  our terminal on the red seaport there  also forced the closure of a pipeline  that carries imported crude to the  capital cartoon  sudan was already facing shortages  stemming from the economic upheaval  caused by the overthrow of president  umar hasan al-bashir  in 2019  muhammad al-jazeera portugal
 

